			"notes": "## What's changed\n\n- Added the **ElytraFly** movement module. With an Elytra equipped, `auto_start` sends one ordinary fall-flying player command as soon as the player is airborne, so gliding begins from a jump with no firework. Control mode flies from the normal movement keys at a configured speed — climbing and descending with Jump and Sneak, hovering with no directional key, and holding position while a screen is open — while Boost mode leaves the vanilla glide alone and only accelerates its existing heading. Vanilla Elytra physics still runs on top of every imposed velocity, durability is consumed normally, and the server remains free to reject, correct, or kick. TooManyHax groups it with the other flight modules.\n- Added a generated blueprint library and the `.build` command. Helikon produces a structure's geometry in code from named parameters and hands it directly to the embedded Baritone builder, so no schematic file is read, written, or downloaded and every design is original Helikon work. The library ships 45 blueprints across eight categories, from geometric primitives and excavation patterns through castles, base utilities, roads, farms, and houses, to a Redeemer statue and a setback skyscraper. Builds are centred on the player, oriented to the way they are looking, and pushed clear of their own footprint; `.build cost` reports the block-by-block material list before anything starts. Twelve material palettes are selectable, and blueprints that only work in one material supply their own.\n- Added a **Build in layers** setting to the Baritone module, defaulting on. Builds now complete each horizontal course from the bottom up rather than working on whichever block happens to be nearest.\n- Added a **Place interval** setting to the Baritone module, defaulting to one tick, roughly quadrupling build and scaffold speed against Baritone's every-fourth-tick default. Servers remain free to rate-limit or reject interactions at any speed.\n- Added a **Block reach** setting to the Baritone module, defaulting to 5.0 against Baritone's own 4.5, so the builder can find a support without walking fully adjacent to it. The setting is bounded at 6.0 and is honest that anti-cheat may flag it.\n- Added **Fast place**, on by default, which removes the dominant cost of a build. Because Minecraft fills a replaceable target in place, an interaction can name the blueprint position itself and needs no supporting block, no walk to a face, no crouch, and no turn to look at it. Helikon fills whatever the player can already reach while Baritone continues pathing and breaking. The pass is bounded per tick, runs only while a `.build` is active, releases the region as soon as the build stops so later player edits are never undone, fills only replaceable positions, skips any position obstructed by a player or mob, and backs off a refused position for a second rather than retrying it every tick.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Full Gradle test, source-style, client-only architecture, checksum, dependency-report, and release-bundle checks passed from a clean build on the tagged commit.\n- GitHub's hosted build and real client GameTest jobs passed for the release commit.\n\n**Full changelog:** https://github.com/erivgout/helikon/compare/v1.9.0...v1.10.0"
		},
